{
  "image_name": "3a44d576fb044ddc6a428044b8880b78.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how skin-resident memory T cells form over time, linking activation in lymphoid tissue to migration through blood and lymph and finally long-term residence in skin. In the priming phase, antigen-presenting cells activate T cells in secondary lymphoid tissue. These activated cells acquire tissue-homing features, including CLA and other trafficking receptors, then enter the blood and move toward skin.\n\nDuring the effector phase, shown as occurring over about 3–10 days, effector T cells encounter antigen-presenting cells and pathogens in skin. Some cells also retain or regain migratory programs associated with circulation, marked by molecules such as S1PR1 and CCR7, indicating a branch toward circulating memory. Another branch produces memory precursor effector cells that remain in tissue. Local signals, especially TGFβ and IL-15, appear to drive these precursors into tissue-resident memory cells.\n\nIn the memory phase, after more than 25 days, both CD8+ and CD4+ TRM cells are shown persisting in skin. The right side highlights a characteristic transcriptional and phenotypic program: reduced T-bet, EOMES, S1PR1, and SELL, with increased RUNX3, FAB4/FAB5, CD69, CD103, BCL-2, and in mice HOBIT and BLIMP1. Overall, the image emphasizes the stepwise differentiation and retention of long-lived skin TRM cells.",
